    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Extrachromosomal DNA (ecDNA) are circularized genomic rearrangements found outside canonical chromosomes, associated with promoting tumorigenesis and shortened survival for patients across cancer types. Given their increasing relevance in cancer progression, there is a need for a more precise understanding of ecDNA structure to inform function. Existing tools to identify and predict ecDNA structures are limited to low throughput, high sensitivity assay-based methods or high throughput, low sensitivity probability-based inference methods using whole-genome sequencing data. Evaluating the impact of ecDNA on tumor progression requires large cohorts with comprehensive clinical follow-up, as is available for large-scale genomics cohorts such as The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) or the International Cancer Genome Consortium (ICGC). Thus, it would be valuable to leverage these substantial sequencing data while improving confidence in ecDNA structural predictions. Here we present ecDNAInspector, a novel tool to refine ecDNA predictions from sequencing data. To characterize and compare ecDNA predictions, ecDNAInspector calculates metrics based on structural features and compares these metrics for an individual cycle against distributions for a user-provided cohort. To enable quality control and filtering of individual cycle predictions, ecDNAInspector reports likely mapping errors at breakends and leverages orthogonal structural variant (SV) calls to validate predicted breakpoints. Unsupervised clustering is then used to identify high-confidence ecDNA cycles. Applying ecDNAInspector to a HER2+ breast tumor cohort, we found a subset of ecDNA cycles with strong SV support and high conservation of oncogene inclusion, including ERBB2, encoding HER2. ecDNAInspector enables higher-confidence utilization of sequencing data predictions in studies of ecDNA structure.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
